1. Variable & Function Terminology
Variable Declaration
When you tell JavaScript that a variable exists without necessarily giving it a value.


    let x;   // Declaration (x exists but value is undefined)
    var y;   // Declaration (y exists but value is undefined)
    Variable Initialization
    When you assign a value to the variable for the first time.


    let x;      // Declaration
    x = 10;     // Initialization
    You can also declare + initialize in one step:


    let x = 10; // Declaration + Initialization
    Variable Redeclaration
    When you declare the same variable name again in the same scope.


    var a = 5;
    var a = 10; // Redeclaration with var ✅ (allowed)

    let b = 5;
    let b = 10; // ❌ Error (let does not allow redeclaration in same scope)


Function Declaration
A named function that is hoisted entirely (you can call it before it appears in the code).


function greet() {
    console.log("Hello");
}
Function Definition
The actual body of the function — what it does.
In JavaScript, a function definition happens when you write the { ... } block for a function.

Declaration + definition happen together for normal functions.

Example:


function sum(a, b) {   // Declaration
    return a + b;      // Definition (body)
}
2. Difference between var, let, and const
Feature	var	let	const
Scope	Function-scoped	Block-scoped	Block-scoped
Redeclaration	✅ Allowed	❌ Not allowed	❌ Not allowed
Reassignment	✅ Allowed	✅ Allowed	❌ Not allowed
Hoisting	Hoisted & initialized with undefined	Hoisted but in Temporal Dead Zone	Hoisted but in Temporal Dead Zone
Best for	Old code, avoid in modern JS	Variables that will change	Constants that never change

Example 1 – Scope

if (true) {
    var a = 10;  // function scope
    let b = 20;  // block scope
    const c = 30; // block scope
}
console.log(a); // 10 ✅
console.log(b); // ❌ ReferenceError
console.log(c); // ❌ ReferenceError
Example 2 – Redeclaration

var x = 1;
var x = 2; // ✅ works

let y = 1;
// let y = 2; // ❌ SyntaxError

const z = 1;
// const z = 2; // ❌ SyntaxError
Example 3 – Reassignment

var a = 5;
a = 10; // ✅

let b = 5;
b = 10; // ✅

const c = 5;
// c = 10; // ❌ TypeError
3. Hoisting
Hoisting = JavaScript moves variable and function declarations to the top of their scope before execution.
But only declarations are hoisted, not initializations.

var Hoisting Example

console.log(a); // undefined (declaration hoisted, value is undefined)
var a = 10;
console.log(a); // 10
let & const Hoisting Example

console.log(b); // ❌ ReferenceError (in Temporal Dead Zone)
let b = 20;

console.log(c); // ❌ ReferenceError (in Temporal Dead Zone)
const c = 30;
Temporal Dead Zone (TDZ) = Time between variable hoisting and initialization when the variable exists but cannot be accessed.

    Function Hoisting

    greet(); // ✅ Works (full function is hoisted)
    function greet() {
        console.log("Hello");
    }

    Function Expression Hoisting

    sayHi(); // ❌ TypeError (variable hoisted but not assigned yet)
    var sayHi = function() {
        console.log("Hi");
    };
    Here, sayHi is treated like a variable — only its name is hoisted, not the function body.

✅ Quick Summary:

    var → Function-scoped, can redeclare, initialized as undefined during hoisting.
    let → Block-scoped, no redeclaration, TDZ applies.
    const → Block-scoped, no redeclaration, must initialize at declaration, TDZ applies.
    Function declarations are fully hoisted; function expressions behave like variables.


    | Keyword | Hoisted? | Initialized in Creation Phase? | TDZ?  | Default Value Before Init |
| ------- | -------- | ------------------------------ | ----- | ------------------------- |
| `var`   | ✅ Yes    | ✅ Yes (`undefined`)            | ❌ No  | `undefined`               |
| `let`   | ✅ Yes    | ❌ No                           | ✅ Yes | N/A (error if accessed)   |
| `const` | ✅ Yes    | ❌ No                           | ✅ Yes | N/A (error if accessed)   |



Key takeaway:

All variables (var, let, const) are hoisted in terms of memory allocation,
but only var gets an immediate default value (undefined) in the creation phase.

let and const stay in the Temporal Dead Zone (TDZ) until execution reaches their declaration line.